<body>
  <main class="container">

    <h1>{title}</h1>

    <div class="meta">
      {author} · {yearDisplay} · {rating} · Review completed
    </div>

    <p>
      <i>Wordslut</i> is evidently meant to invite controversy both through its
      subject and its discussion and use of the profane. What claims to be a
      map to feminist and intersectional language instead resembles a tourist
      brochure: colorful, selective, and of limited use once the journey begins.
    </p>

    <p>
      Montell’s main argument is that almost all human societies have been
      organized as patriarchies due to language. Because the language one
      speaks influences thought, the “patriarchy” is reinforced by the language
      we use. She writes, “One of our culture’s biggest obstacles is the idea
      that language doesn’t matter the way that other more tangible forms of
      freedom and oppression do.”
    </p>

    <p>
      Montell’s evidence is highly suspect, relying heavily on anecdotes from
      feminist professors from the 1970s and 1990s, polls of her followers and
      friends who are likely to share her views, occasional isolated studies,
      and a lack of bibliography. Montell presents many examples of sexist
      language and sexist societies, but examples alone do not establish that
      language causes or substantially reinforces sexism.
    </p>

    <p>
      Even if language influences thought, Montell never demonstrates that
      linguistic reform should be prioritized over more tangible reforms such as
      changes in law, education, or economic opportunity. Indeed, she
      acknowledges that language itself is shaped by the culture that speaks
      it. If culture and language influence one another, then reforming
      institutions and social norms should naturally produce linguistic change
      as well. The book never establishes that changing language is the more
      effective starting point.
    </p>

    <p>
      Montell may have more convincingly demonstrated a scientific interest in
      her thesis had she compared linguistic practices across cultures with
      measures of gender equality. Such an analysis would have required
      grappling with difficult empirical questions, including the Nordic
      paradox: why some of the world's most gender-egalitarian societies
      continue to exhibit persistent sex differences in occupations and
      preferences despite progressive social norms. At minimum, this suggests
      that language alone cannot bear the explanatory weight Montell places
      upon it. Norwegian, for example, contains grammatical gender despite
      Norway being among the world's most gender-equal societies.
    </p>

    <p>
      Specifically, she argues:
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      “In French, for instance, most prestigious job titles are masculine. The
      French words for police officer, doctor, professor, engineer, politician,
      lawyer, surgeon, and dozens of others all have masculine gender.”
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      This is an oversimplification. Modern French contains feminine forms of
      many professions: <i>un avocat</i> and <i>une avocate</i> (lawyer),
      <i>un chirurgien</i> and <i>une chirurgienne</i> (surgeon), and
      <i>un infirmier</i> and <i>une infirmière</i> (nurse). While linguistic
      conventions may reflect historical social structures, they do not by
      themselves prove that grammar maintains those structures.
    </p>

    <p>
      Montell also attacks social perceptions of occupations, describing the
      harmful “pervasive assumption that many esteemed professions, surgeons,
      scientists, lawyers, writers, actors are male while models, nurses, and
      prostitutes are female.” She links these perceptions to implicit biases
      that maintain occupational segregation and unequal treatment.
    </p>

    <p>
      However, occupational segregation is not necessarily evidence of unequal
      treatment. Countries such as Norway consistently rank among the world's
      most gender-equal societies while continuing to exhibit substantial
      occupational segregation. Furthermore, many of these perceptions
      correspond to current workplace demographics. Registered nurses remain
      overwhelmingly female, while surgeons and lawyers remain
      disproportionately male. The existence of statistically accurate
      expectations does not establish that language or sexism against womenhere created these patterns, nor
      does it demonstrate that changing language will substantially alter them.
    </p>

    <p>
      Montell introduces several interesting etymologies, such as those of
      <i>dude</i> and <i>guy</i>, both of which originated as words referring
      specifically to men but are now frequently used in gender-neutral ways.
      She nevertheless advocates replacing such terms with alternatives like
      <i>folks</i>, arguing that their continued use reinforces male
      normativity.
    </p>

    <p>
      This argument places too much weight on etymology and too little on
      contemporary usage. Words routinely diverge from their historical
      origins. Few English speakers associate <i>villain</i> with its original
      meaning of peasant or serf, <i>nice</i> with its original meaning of
      foolish, or <i>decimate</i> with its original meaning of reducing by
      one-tenth. Likewise, for many speakers, <i>guys</i> and <i>dudes</i>
      function simply as informal collective terms rather than direct
      references to men.
    </p>

    <p>
      It was genuinely surprising, however, how many words for women have
      pejorated over time. I did not know the origins or historical usage of
      terms such as “hussy” and had not noticed the broader pattern. However,
      the argument for language reform remains weak. Montell largely assumes
      that the derogation of female terms demonstrates language's causal role
      in maintaining sexism. Another plausible explanation is that these words
      became derogatory because of existing societal attitudes, and short-term
      reforms may eventually succumb to the euphemism treadmill. If the latter
      explanation is correct, changing vocabulary alone is unlikely to
      eliminate the underlying prejudice.
    </p>

    <p>
      The existence of gender differences does not by itself demonstrate
      discrimination. A complete explanation must separate the effects of
      social pressure, historical conditions, economic incentives, biological
      differences, and individual preferences. If a theory explains every
      gender difference primarily through patriarchy, it risks becoming
      unfalsifiable because any outcome can be interpreted as evidence of the
      same cause.
    </p>

  </main>
</body>
</html>